Running from a checkout
-----------------------

bin/emcl runs Emacs Common Lisp from the command line, much like sbcl:

  bin/emcl                              start a REPL
  bin/emcl --load foo.lisp --eval '(main)' --non-interactive
  bin/emcl --script foo.lisp            run a script (#! line allowed)
  bin/emcl --help                       list all options

It uses the `emacs' on your PATH; set EMACS to use another one.  emcl can
be symlinked into a directory on your PATH.

Inside Emacs
------------

Load lisp/load-cl.el, then type "M-x emacs-cl" for a listener buffer.

Testing
-------

Run "make check" (or lisp/test) to run the test suite.

Note: byte-compiling the system ("make", i.e. M-x compile-cl) currently
produces .elc files that fail to load; run from source until that's fixed.
